"Summarizes a startup's risky business-model assumptions with explicit attention to problems, solutions, metrics and defensibility.\n\nUse when: Compare early venture concepts before substantial investment. Choose which business assumptions to investigate next.\n\nAvoid when: A detailed delivery architecture or a fully validated financial model is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Nine blocks emphasize problem, solution, value proposition, advantage, customers, channels, metrics, costs and revenue. Existing alternatives and early adopters refine relevant blocks. Evidence markers distinguish current learning from initial guesses.\n\nBuild: Choose a specific customer and a small set of important problems. State existing alternatives before proposing the smallest plausible solution. Complete economic and acquisition assumptions, then prioritize learning.\n\nCheck: The problem is expressed from the customer's situation. Unfair advantage is defensible rather than a generic aspiration. Metrics can test the central business assumptions.\n\nPitfalls: Describing a feature list while leaving customer pain vague. Calling enthusiasm or a first-mover claim an enduring advantage.\n\nExample: A neighborhood tool-rental venture identifies occasional household projects, existing borrowing habits and a deposit-based offer to test locally.\n\nAlternatives: Business Model Canvas, Test card."
